Here's how I'd build that controller method, following the discovery-first workflow.

## First, check what the project already does

Before writing it, look at `composer.json` for the framework version, then read one or two neighboring controllers to see:
- Are Form Requests already used for validation, or is `$request->validate([...])` the house style?
- Is there an `app/Actions` or `app/Services` layer, or does logic live on the model?

Match whatever's there rather than introducing a new pattern for one method. I'll write this assuming Form Requests, which is the most common Laravel setup.

## Make the Form Request first

```php
// app/Http/Requests/StoreOrderRequest.php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Requests;

use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\FormRequest;

class StoreOrderRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize(): bool
    {
        // Tighten this to the real rule, e.g. $this->user()->can('create', Order::class)
        return $this->user() !== null;
    }

    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [
            'customer_id'  => ['required', 'integer', 'exists:customers,id'],
            'notes'        => ['nullable', 'string', 'max:1000'],
            'items'        => ['required', 'array', 'min:1'],
            'items.*.sku'  => ['required', 'string', 'max:64'],
            'items.*.qty'  => ['required', 'integer', 'min:1'],
        ];
    }
}
```

## Then a thin controller method

```php
// app/Http/Controllers/OrderController.php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Http\Requests\StoreOrderRequest;
use App\Actions\CreateOrder;
use App\Models\Order;
use Illuminate\Http\RedirectResponse;

class OrderController extends Controller
{
    public function store(StoreOrderRequest $request, CreateOrder $createOrder): RedirectResponse
    {
        $order = $createOrder->handle($request->validated());

        return redirect()
            ->route('orders.show', $order)
            ->with('status', 'Order created.');
    }
}
```

Two things worth noting about that method:

**Validation is already done** by the time it runs — `StoreOrderRequest` fails with a 422 (or redirects back with errors on a web route) before the controller body executes.

**`validated()` only, never `all()`.** Build the model from the exact fields the form declared, so a stray `is_admin` or `status` in the payload can't be mass-assigned.

## Where the actual rule lives

The controller shouldn't contain business logic. If the project has an action layer, put it there:

```php
// app/Actions/CreateOrder.php
<?php

namespace App\Actions;

use App\Models\Order;

class CreateOrder
{
    public function handle(array $data): Order
    {
        $order = Order::create([
            'customer_id' => $data['customer_id'],
            'notes'       => $data['notes'] ?? null,
        ]);

        foreach ($data['items'] as $item) {
            $order->items()->create([
                'sku' => $item['sku'],
                'qty' => $item['qty'],
            ]);
        }

        return $order;
    }
}
```

If the codebase has no action layer and logic lives on the model, wrap the above in a static `Order::createFromRequestData()` instead — but keep it out of the controller either way.

## The model side

```php
// app/Models/Order.php
final class Order extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = ['customer_id', 'notes'];

    protected function casts(): array
    {
        return [
            'created_at' => 'datetime',
            'updated_at' => 'datetime',
        ];
    }

    public function customer(): BelongsTo
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Customer::class);
    }

    public function items(): HasMany
    {
        return $this->hasMany(OrderItem::class);
    }
}
```

`$fillable` is declared explicitly — never leave both `$fillable` and `$guarded` unset. (Use `casts()` as a method on Laravel 11+; on older apps use the `$casts` property.)

## Route

```php
// routes/web.php
Route::post('/orders', [OrderController::class, 'store'])
    ->middleware('auth')
    ->name('orders.store');
```

## If this touches a queue

Not needed for the create itself, but if you fan out to a job (e.g. sending an order confirmation), remember Laravel queues are **at-least-once** — a retried job will run again. Guard the side effect before you ship it:

```php
final class SendOrderConfirmation implements ShouldQueue, ShouldBeUnique
{
    public function uniqueId(): string
    {
        return (string) $this->order->id;
    }
}
```

or do an explicit dedupe check inside `handle()`. Don't defer this because "it basically never retries" — the cost of guarding it now is small against a duplicated email in production.

## Before you call it done

Run the project's own gates:

```bash
php artisan test          # or ./vendor/bin/pest / ./vendor/bin/phpunit
./vendor/bin/phpstan analyse   # if Larastan is configured
./vendor/bin/pint              # if configured
```

And sanity-check the four things that trip people up here: every touched model has `$fillable`/`$guarded`, the input flows through validation before use, no relationship is accessed in a loop without an eager load, and any queued side effect is idempotent.

